### Use Cases and Practices for Intent-Based Networking
* Presentation: [2 - Use Cases and Practices for Intent-Based Networking](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmrg-sessa-2-use-cases-and-practices-for-intent-based-networking-00)
* Presenter: Je-Hoon Park

* Je-Hoon Park reviewed updates incorporated into the IBN use cases document over the past three months following expert feedback:
  * Expanded sections on data collection, module naming, policy conflict handling, intent reporting, green intent, and explainable AI (XAI).
  * Refined Software-Defined Vehicle (SDV) management aspects, updating the SDV intent lifecycle diagram and incorporating safety/security considerations.
  * Clarified grouping of network service functions across specific use cases, including i2NSF, 5G network automation, 5G V2X energy management, segment routing, SLAs, and cloud security.
  * Introduced human-in-the-loop concepts to mitigate LLM hallucinations and addressed AI agent governance and accountability.
* **Discussion**:
  * Sheng Wu asked how vendor-specific configuration statements would be verified under this model.
  * Je-Hoon Park responded that intent verification evaluates translated policy impacts and potential security exposures, but standardized verification remains an open research topic, underlining the need for AI agent governance.
  * Document Shepherd (Jéferson Nobre / chair) stated the document is mature and recommended moving forward with Research Group Last Call (RGLC) on the mailing list.

### Priority-Based Data Transfer Control for LLM Inference Streaming Leveraging Prompt Importance
* Presentation: [3 - Priority-Based Data Transfer Control for LLM Inference Streaming Leveraging Prompt Importance (IEEE NetSoft 2026)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmrg-sessa-3-priority-based-data-transfer-control-for-llm-inference-streaming-leveraging-prompt-importance-ieee-netsoft-2026-00)
* Presenter: Hiroki Baba

* Hiroki Baba presented research addressing network latency and TCP head-of-line (HOL) blocking in interactive LLM token streaming over unreliable wireless networks.
* Proposed an AI communication control proxy architecture operating between client and inference server:
  1. Classifies request importance into classes (high, medium, low).
  2. Maps request importance to HTTP/3 stream priorities (urgency levels 1, 3, 6; incremental parameters).
  3. Maps stream priorities to transport scheduling and network QoS mechanisms (DSCP markings: EF, AF31, Best Effort; 5G QFI flows).
* Prototype evaluations using an Ollama GPTOSS 20B backend demonstrated that HTTP/3 over QUIC maintained significantly lower Time to First Token (TTFT) and tail token latency (95th percentile) compared to HTTP/2 over TCP under high packet loss (up to 30%) and delay conditions (up to 100 ms).
* **Discussion**:
  * Esteban Carisimo inquired about the HTTP/3 library used; Hiroki Baba confirmed the prototype used `aioquic`, though the proposal is library-agnostic.
  * Dirk Kutscher (IRTF Chair) asked about specific network management research challenges. Hiroki Baba highlighted configuration, QoS monitoring, and QoS assurance designed for LLM user-experience metrics.
  * Yong-Geun Hong asked how backend LLM processing latency is accounted for relative to propagation delay. Hiroki Baba explained the study focused on wireless network degradation, assuming compute-layer latency-guarantee mechanisms would address backend compute delays.
  * Chang Zhou asked if the approach is applicable to non-mobile networks. Hiroki Baba confirmed that the mapping between application priority and QoS applies to fixed network environments as well.

### Network Digital Twin and Agentic AI based Architecture for AI-driven Network Operations
* Presentation: [4 - Network Digital Twin and Agentic AI based Architecture for AI-driven Network Operations](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmrg-sessa-4-network-digital-twin-and-agentic-ai-based-architecture-for-ai-driven-network-operations-01)
* Presenter: Sheng Wu

* Sheng Wu presented updates to draft version 06 on NDT and Agentic AI architecture for network operations.
* Detailed the core functional components:
  * Agent Gateway: Manages registration, security, and human-in-the-loop controls.
  * NDT Sandbox: Simulates network topology, performance, and traffic built upon the NMRG NDT architecture.
  * Hybrid Agent System: Supports multi-agent communication and distinguishes persistent network agents from on-demand task agents.
  * Agent Fabric: Integrates agent gateways to provide observability, guardrail supervision, metrics logging, execution tracing, anomaly detection, and human escalation/intervention.
* Key changes in v06 include moving registration/team-formation to an appendix, converting normative RFC 2119 language to informative text, and defining single-domain vs. cross-domain integration.
* Highlighted hackathon implementation results demonstrating agent policy enforcement, dynamic observability data models, execution trajectory diagnosis, and streaming sanction handovers.
* Presenter requested Research Group adoption.
* **Discussion**:
  * Jérôme François (Co-chair) noted that the chairs will evaluate the draft against NMRG adoption criteria—specifically focusing on clearly defined research questions—and will share the adoption checklist with the authors.

### Data Generation and Optimization for Network Digital Twin
* Presentation: [5 - Data Generation and Optimization for Network Digital Twin](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmrg-sessa-5-data-generation-and-optimization-for-network-digital-twin-00)
* Presenter: Chang Zhou

* Chang Zhou presented a three-stage closed-loop framework addressing NDT data challenges (rare anomaly data shortages, strict privacy restrictions, and variable data quality):
  1. Data Generation: Synthesizes topology, routing, and traffic metrics using packet-level simulators (NS-3, Mininet++), flow-level simulators (FlowSim, AstroSim), and Generative AI (GANs, VAEs, diffusion models).
  2. Data Optimization: Seed sample selection (feature extraction, clustering, expert verification) and incremental optimization (categorizing into regular, hard, and out-of-distribution samples).
  3. Data Assessment: Evaluates datasets across six quality dimensions using statistical tests (QQ plots, KS tests), protocol constraints, and downstream task model evaluation.
* Showcased evaluation results from the AutoPT framework (ITU-T AI/ML challenge benchmark), reaching competitive delay estimation accuracy (~8.6% MAPE) using only 100 optimized samples compared to baseline approaches requiring over 200,000 samples.
* **Discussion**:
  * Jérôme François (Co-chair) asked what elements of the framework are specific to NDT compared to general ML data generation pipelines.
  * Chang Zhou explained that NDT requires physics-grounded synthetic data matching physical network distributions, and the framework aims to reduce sample size requirements across multiple downstream NDT models. Jérôme François suggested continuing technical discussions on the mailing list.

